 - **Symptom:** `[RED] sf: the game connects to service auddmg:u itself, which the host does not serve`, then `game called nn::diag abort: Result 0xa99` at `sdk+0x1f61ac` under `audio::ListVirtualInputDevices`.
 - **Cause:** the game lists the virtual input devices when its audio starts (`main+0xf48124`) and opens the first with `audio::OpenInputStream` if there is one. The list goes through a service the host does not serve.
-- **Fix:** `ListVirtualInputDevices` reports none (`hle_audio.c` `h_is_List`), and the game skips its input stream.
+- **Fix:** `ListVirtualInputDevices` reports none (`hle_audio.c` `h_is_List`), and the game skips its input stream. With `DM_MIC=1` (Mac only) it lists one device and the input streams capture the Mac's default input through one Audio Queue, started with the first started stream and stopped with the last; `GetTrueRawAudioInputDevice` names the same microphone, each stream keeps a ring of its capacity (the oldest frames go when the game reads late), and the app's Info.plist carries `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` (`dm/mac/pack_app.py`), so macOS asks the player at the first capture. Ocarina of Time opens two 48 kHz mono PCM16 streams of 4,800 frames (`main+0xf48168`, `main+0xf481a8`) and reads them on its audio thread (`main+0x336d510`) for the ocarina played into the microphone. A Mac's input arrives as the microphone gives it, quieter than what a Switch's system hands a game (Ocarina of Time said its volume was too low): the frames get a gain that follows the loudest of the last seconds, up to 64, or `DM_MIC_GAIN`'s; a gain that brought each 10 ms period to the target raised the room's noise between notes to the notes' level. Every 5 s of capture the log gives the input's peak and RMS, the gain, and the frames each stream's reader took and lost; `DM_MIC_DUMP` writes what the game reads.
 - **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (SDK 22.2.8); Star Fox imports the same calls.
 
